- The user has authorized you to set review states: do not ask for permission, and do not offer to "set it separately if needed".
- The state is required for any verdict based on a commit you reviewed — in this turn or in an earlier one, it does not matter. That commit is `<SHA>`: the one the prompt listed for this PR, otherwise the `headRefOid` you recorded when you started. Publish the state now, only as `gh api repos/<owner>/<repo>/pulls/<N>/reviews -f commit_id=<SHA> -f event=<APPROVE|REQUEST_CHANGES> -f body=…`, with `<SHA>` written out as a literal 40-hex value (a hook denies `gh pr review --approve | --request-changes`, a missing or non-literal `commit_id`, and a `commit_id` other than the recorded one).
- Request changes (🔴) always goes on `<SHA>`. An Approve (🟢) in an epic run goes on `<SHA>`. A manual Approve goes out only if `gh pr view <N> -R <owner/repo> --json headRefOid` still returns `<SHA>`; if the head moved, set no status and say in the reply which commits arrived unreviewed.
- When the prompt listed cold reviewer findings with ids, the review body must carry one line per id with its verdict (fixed | open | refuted) — an approve included. Pass the body in the command itself (`-f body="…"`, or a heredoc): the hook does not read a body from a file (`-F body=@file`, `--input`).
- If GitHub rejects the POST (e.g. the commit is no longer in the PR, or you are the PR author), set no status, say why plainly in the reply and do not republish on another commit. The only legitimate reasons to end without a status are this and a manual Approve over a moved head.
- Then repeat your full verdict reply as your final message, its first line starting with 🟢 or 🔴 — only the final message reaches the user.
